Herman Z. Resto (hresto@execpc.com) wrote:
: mouse is usually linked to /dev/cua1. I fixed the problem by removing ("rm
: mouse") the mouse link in /dev/mouse and relinking it like this: "ln -s
: /dev/ttys0 /dev/mouse"

If you would really do that you would end up with a dead mouse as
/dev/ttys0 is not what you need (even if it exists on you machine).
You want /dev/ttyS0  (major 4, minor 64) if you had mouse on a serial
port 0 (a.k.a. COM1). /dev/cua? entries still exist in 2.2 kernels but
you will get nastygram on every open reminding you that they will be
gone in the future.  If this was really /dev/cua1 then /dev/ttyS0 will
be a wrong anyway.

Most Alphas have ps/2 mice anyway so you may really want /dev/psaux
for your mouse.

  --mj




