>> Is it possible to boot directly off a CD to install Linux? (I can
>> install NT like this (and apparently DEC Unix as well) but I can't
>> seem to find any info. on Linux.........)

>	It is usually a BIOS option, and I haven't seen anything on the UDB
>that would allow a boot from CD.  I was thinking about trying to use the FMU
>to re-write the flash w/MILO, but you would still need a floppy for that,
>unless the fmu.gz is somewhere on the CD.

>	I am talking nonsense now eheh.  I dont think that there is a way,
>but I could be wrong :)

If linload.exe and the apropriate MILO are somewhere om the cd in plain format
(i.e. other than in the floppy-image) you should be able to set up a
bootselection to get MILO running. Once at the MILO-prompt say:
boot scd1:/kernels/noname.gz root=/dev/scd1

Alternatively, you can install NT (brrr...) with a small system-partition (FAT)
and a larger one for the OS (NTFS or FAT). Use NT to put MILO and linload.exe in
the small FAT partition. Then set up a bootselection and continue as above. Once
you're in the RedHat installation you may wipe the larger NT partition.

(now that I come to think of it, I'm not sure what tools there are to extract
linload and MILO from the floppy-image, other than rawrite, which needs a
floppy. You might need to do that on another machine, and use the network to get
it on the multia)

Groeten,
Geerten.




