That just *has* to be slow -  Wabi on an Intel based-box is pretty
reasonable, since all there is to translate are system calls (the
x86 code is passed right to the x86 CPU), on a SPARC box
(one of the original ports of WABI), speed suffered since the
software not only mapped system calls but *every* x86 instruction.

Emulating a CISC CPU on a RISC CPU is expensive (time-wise),
since few instructions cleanly map from the CISC CPU to the RISC
CPU (it may take several RISC CPU instructions to emulate one
CISC instruction), and then there is the processing needed to DO
the translation, so you *may* (and this is a pure guess) see as many
as twenty or more instructions on the RISC machine to perform the
work of the CISC CPU, c ausing your effective PC speed to be a
fraction of the clock speed of the CPU that is doing the emulation.

For example, a 533 Mhz CPU might feel like a 26 Mhz Pentium?!

Instead of buying a copy of Caldera WABI, you may be better of
getting a cheap intel box ($100-200 should get you a usable
Pentium system, say P75 - P133 with some RAM & HD) and put
Windows on that, and if you want better integration with the alpha
you could run Linux on the box with Caldera Wabi on it, exporting
the display onto your alpha...

Just my thoughts,

Ken
khansen@njcc.com

