Joseph Sarkes wrote:
> 
> Jack Twilley (jmt+usenet@nycap.rr.com) wrote:
> : >>>>> "Rick" == Rick Brice <rwbrice@mar.lmco.com> writes:
> 
> :     Rick> Why not a big drive on the internal ide....Toshiba makes
> :     Rick> them.  Regards, Rick
> 
> : From what I've read, one can't use IDE and the PCI option.
> 
> that isn't a problem... I have an ide drive in my multia here
> which works fine, along with a pci ethernet card.
> 
> The conflict comes with internal 3.5 inch hard drives. They take up
> the space that the internal single pci card would go. In addition, the
> boxes outfitted for internal 3.5 inch scsi drives don't have the pci
> connector installed on the scsi/riser card. (you CAN solder on on if
> you decide you want to, just don't ever expect to fit a 3.5 inch drive
> inside afterwards...
> 
> The only glitchs with the ide drive in the multia that  I have seen are:
> 
> 1) very slow, no faster than ethernet... If you have an nfs server
> available, that may be the way to go.
> 
> 2) takes a weird cable, although there have been sources for them
> mentioned here from time to time.
> 
> 3) you HAVE to boot using arc software and NOT srm to use the ide on
> the multia as a boot device. perhaps you could also burn milo into
> flash but I haven't done that, so can't say for certain. This also
> implies that you have a dos fat partition to boot milo from, as arc
> can't read ext2fs.
> 
> The internal ide drive DOES make the multia a standalone system, and
> the drive i have is about 3Gb, which is enough to do the job. HOWEVER
> 
> 4) if you get bad sectors on the drive, there is no way using the multia
> to low level format the drive to an errorless state again (at least as
> far as I am aware) The best bet to handle the errors is to reformat the
> offending partition using bad block scanning to map out the bad sectors.
> You then can't reliably read the entire partition if you so desired, for
> backup or whatever. Things go weird when you get bad blocks, timeouts,
> lockups, etc.  At least scsi has bad block remapping done inside the
> harddrive. If ide supports this, I haven't seen any documentation about
> it, or any utilities for linux to format ide drives low level, do
> diagnostics, etc.
> 
> : That's the primary concern here.
> 
> : Jack.
