I went through the hassle of benchmarking the f77bench.tar.gz

with libcpml.a in order to compare any possible improvement over

libffm.a on Linux/Alpha (-O3 -ffast-math -funroll-loops -mcpu=21164a

on LX-4 Samsung board with 128MB @533MHz).

  Below, the first column was obtained with

Intel/Linux on P-II @ 400 Mhz using Absoft f77 version 4.4.

The result for this part was made with the

-O and -B100 compilation switches.

           APF77    g77libffm    g77libcpml

AC        16.72    47.23    24.45

ADI        11.10    16.31    16.39

AIR        (NaN)      48.31    44.05

CHESS        54.47    51.34    51.23

DODUC        100.13    88.76    82.29

LM8        24.41    29.49    29.06

LP8        19.55    Alpha/Linux has a trouble due to the clock problem.

MDB        13.19    12.36    12.37

MOLENRGY    57.88    60.75    60.92

PI        29.32    151.97    43.47

PNPOLY    13.43    16.97    16.84

RO        36.47    62.56    65.87

SM4        20.00    14.09    13.70

TFFT        54.47    50.79    58.13

WH4        44.68    45.76    41.84

All the timing numbers were obtained for "real" not for "user".

Both Absoft compilers (f77/f90) have trouble with AIR.f

Don't expect Absoft's f90 would give something similar to the above

APF77 result.  They should do some homework with their f90.

Is Digital Fortran for Alpha/Linux still too far a way?  When can such

a thing see the light of the day?

Best regards,

G. Hugh Song

