You probably won't see any noticeable difference in performance in most
applications between libcpml and libffm.  libcpml is faster in most cases,
but not enough so that it would show up in typical applications.  What
libcpml does give you is "better answers" (hey, some computer company should
use that as a slogan...) than libffm at the same speed (more or less).
libcpml is also complete, where libffm is not.  libcpml, like libffm, is
much faster than the distribution libm.

In the notes describing libcpml are the results of doing actual routine
timing, along with error bounds. I think your benchmark results bear out the
claims for libcpml.

