Christian Hennecke <christian.hennecke@ruhr-uni-bochum.de> wrote:
: I am completely new to the Alpha and Linux world. I installed Redhat 5.2
: on a Digital XL 266. Now there's a severe problem with the time
: settings. When installing I selected the system clock to show GMT and
: also checked the NT ARC console option. I guess that wasn't so good...

: Upon startup the NT ARC console complains about an inconsistency in the
: time setting which has to be fixed. I can see that the date and time are
: ok, but the day is wrong (one day later than it should be, i.e. Sunday
: instead of Saturday). The problem is that I can't change the time
: setting. It doesn't seem to matter what I type in, the !&*# thing always
: says "Error: Invalid time or date blabla". Well, without the time
: setting fixed the system refuses to boot... Aarrggghhh!

: Sadly, I don't have a manual for the XL 266. Can I delete the clock
: settings somehow? There's a "clear CMOS" jumper on the motherboard, but

There  is  no  jumper,  and  if  you  try to reset the realtime clock by
connecting the pins together you will have the year ~2198 ;-)

I  going throuth this some weeks ago with my xl266. A very nice guy from
DEC send me a patched linload.exe which loads automaticly a file  called
milo  from the same floppy. If you were in milo you can start linux. Set
the time with date and "burn" the linux time with hwclock  -A  into  the
Bios realtime clock. See for both the manpages!! 

The patched linload.exe is on

        ftp.ub.uni-marburg.de/pub/unix/linux/alpha/linload_auto.exe

You  can  start  it  with  the  ARC menu "run a program" or rename it to
XLUPDATE.EXE and use the firmware update option. Use your normal milo.

Juergen




