I wrote on Feb. 26th:
> Our new shiny XP1000 has arrived (as an upgrade of a Miata (PW500a)).
> A quick try at using the Miata MILO with AlphaBIOS gave a hang right
> after switching the PAL code (not really a surprise).  There appears
> to be no MILO for this box, so the next thing we will look at is
> booting a generic kernel with aboot from the SRM console.

We have now done this, and it works nicely (well, we had one floppy
read error, but that was easily fixed by reformatting it).

What we did was:

We took aboot-0.5-7.src.rpm, unpacked it, applied all the supplied
patches, and built it.  Then we put it on a floppy with e2writeboot.
The we mounted the floppy and put a kernel on it (we used a generic
2.2.1 kernel (BTW, generic kernels are the best thing since
Linux-Alpha)). Then we put the floppy into the XP1000, booted aboot
with

boot dva0

and then the kernel. This worked up to mounting the root fs (which we
had not installed), so we then took the hard disk from the Miata and
put it into the XP1000.  And then we could boot with

vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2

We had to fix the time (we had booted the Miata with AlphaBIOS/ARC),
but apart from that it seems to work.

The docs we used were the SRM-HOWTO and aboot's README and INSTALL
files.

Sorry, I cannot tell you how to do it, if you have to install from
scratch.

The only remaining thing would be to get an automatic boot from the
hard disk.  However, I think this won't be possible, because the hard
disk is partitioned with the PC partitioning scheme.

