I'm counting this as a victory... I just compiled my first working kernel,
including support for the NetGear FA310TX 10/100 ethernet board (uses a
tulip-flavored driver, but *not* the one that came with Linux/Alpha!)

Second try, though. The first try used the default tulip driver, and it
didn't work with the NetGear, to the point of going into kernel panic.
Fortunately, the HOWTO I was following detailed how to set LILO to use
either kernel. Simple trick to make those directions work for ARC (just
create a new boot option, passing the "other" kernel info to MILO).

So far, so good. I wish it were easier to find this kind of information,
though. If using Windows 95/98/NT were building things with Duplo blocks,
working with Linux/Alpha is kind of like going out and picking up scrap
sheet metal, a torch, some pliers, shears... you get the idea.

Now for ipfwadm and some time to play... Both ethernet interfaces are
working, with dissimilar IP addresses on two separate "networks"
consisting of a Mac on one port with a crossover cable, and a PC on the
other port with another crossover cable.

The Alpha can see the internet via the Mac (running IPNetRouter with NAT
active), but the PC can't get through (yet!)

Things are now getting a bit complicated. Should be easier when I pick up
the switch next week, and I can get rid of the crossover cables (timing
while powering up is a bit tricky in order to detect the 100BT - otherwise
it defaults back to 10BT.)

