It just so happens that 2^24 = 16Mb. This leads me to believe that
the default stack size is 16Mb. Since other variables are also on the
stack (some which you may not be aware of implicitly, e.g. argc, argv, etc.)
you will always have < 16Mb for your local variables. Unless you are
willing to right your code in assembler or  patch the compiler to allocate
more stack by default this is going to be a limitation. The second
probably would not be wise since most applications do not use
1Mb in local variables. A better alternative is to dynamically allocate
memory in the exact amounts needed using malloc or calloc. Refer
to the man pages for a description. Here is a simple example.

>#include <stdio.h>
>#include <stdlib.h>
>
>int main()
>    {
>    char *ptr;
>
>    ptr = (char *) malloc(1<<24);
>    if(ptr == NULL) printf("Failed\n");
>    else
>        {
>        printf("Sucess\n");
>        free(ptr);
>        }
>    return 0;
>    }
>
>I have 96 Mb and this worked fine.
>For string variables, I prefer calloc since it sets the buffer to zero thus
>guaranteeing
>a NULL terminated string. This assumes you have at least one extra byte.
>
>
>Owen Peter Vajk wrote in message <36C208BE.1D15@leland.Stanford.EDU>...
>>Is there a maximum size for an array in C on an alpha?  I have a program
>>that uses a character array that's about 27 MB, on an alpha with 128 MB
>>of RAM.  I compile it under gcc, and when I run it I get a segmentation
>>fault immediately, even before it hits my first debug print statement.
>>Any suggestions?  The limit seems to be a bit below 2^23 characters.
