Rick Brice (rbrice@xspam.gelac.mar.lmco.com) wrote:
: ECC SIMMS. 9-Chip or 18-chip SIMMs will work.
: The 8-chip and the 16-chip "TRUE PARITY" stuff the PC guys sell will NOT work!

: Rick

Hmmmm, seems that terms are being REDEFINED here. Parity simms were the 
9 bit wide or 36 bit wide simms. The NON-PARITY simms were the cheaper
simms that became prevalent when motherboard manufacturers started looking
for ways to shave costs, and left out parity support. I think this happened
on some of the later 486 motherboards and with the pentiums. It wasn't until
the triton II chip set that parity became usefull again, as that motherboard
supported both memory parity checking and ecc using parity memory. 

Another cost shaving method that came into being was a FAKE parity simm
that contained 8 or 32 bit wide parity memory and parity generating 
circuitry, either as separate chips or built into the ram chips themselves,
to fake out the motherboard. These chips can not store the extra ecc parity
memory bits, and will cause ecc errors/not work. 

Hamming parity generation is actually (for 64 bits of memory) basically
7 different sets of parity generation using different bits of the memory
such that the memory error causes parity errors on multiple bits. These
bits are used to address the offending memory bit and toggle it, thus
correcting the error. There is an 8th bit of parity used to parity check
the 7 parity bits, in the case that it was one of those that lost data.
I'm not sure about the total bit mapping used, but that is basically how
ecc memory works, doing exclusive ors on sets of memory bits in the 64 bit
wide word.

That being the case, it is obvious that no parity bits, or fake parity 
bits will NOT do anything for you. In years past, there were special
ecc simms, in addition to the regular parity simms. Since ecc is done
on the entire word, individual chip selects were not needed on the 4
9 bit wide memory blocks on the simm. Thus ecc simms would not work
in say an old 486 motherboard as you could not write to a single byte
of memory, but had to write the full 32 bit wide location. Since to
set the ecc bits the original data needed to be read in its entirety 
and the parity generated using the old and new data (however many bytes
were being written) and then the entire word written back, individual 
byte selects were redundant, and more expensive. Parity memory also
commonly needed two different size memory chips as two 4 bit wide X 1 Meg
chips would be used with a 1 Meg x 1 bit chip for parity, for each byte
of storage (multiply by 4 for the 72 pin simm) thus 4 X 1 Meg chips and 
8 X  4 meg chips for a 4 megabyte parity simm. (12 chips total on the simm)

I hope I haven't offended anyone with my long windedness.




--
Joseph Sarkes                   mailto:joe@mediaone.net



