>timmons@ovpr.uga.edu wrote:
><SNIP>
>> I've noticed this on my box at home...  Which nic driver are you using?
>I'm using tulip driver...
>
>> I was using the tulip driver initially, but if you look at the source code
>> for it, the author says to use the de4x5 driver unless it doesn't work
>> with your card.  Hence, I recently recompiled the kernel to use the
>> de4x5...  It's only been up for a day, but the network hasn't slowed down
>> yet.
>Another kind soul has replied and suggested an upgrade of the latest
>tulip driver (v.90). The tulip driver code has apparently forked and
>even the latest tulip driver from kernel (2.2.2) is from a different
>codebase from the developer (Donald Becker)...
>
>I think I'll try that first, failing which I try the de4x5 driver...


I had a LOT of trouble with the tulip driver (v.89) with my AS 200/233.
I had real slow-down problems transferring to the machine, and
various disconnect and hang problems.  I also couldn't make it
"see" my network hub (I can't remember the details, but it was some
sort of media detection problem).  I ended up replacing the hub (an old
el-cheepo Boca thing -- which worked fine for my PCs, to a new el-cheepo
Bay/NetGear thing) and switching to the de4x5 driver.  Now everything
works great.

I never figured out what my tulip problems stemmed from.

    - Matt Goheen





